WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.270 --> 00:00:02.040
- Trump gets a special master.

00:00:02.040 --> 00:00:03.420
It's the most beautiful,

00:00:03.420 --> 00:00:05.430
special master anyone has ever seen.

00:00:05.430 --> 00:00:06.810
It's the most special.

00:00:06.810 --> 00:00:11.250
The most wonderful, special
master that has ever existed.

00:00:11.250 --> 00:00:12.083
He's not wrong.

00:00:12.083 --> 00:00:14.310
People have never seen a
special master like this,

00:00:14.310 --> 00:00:15.690
but what does that all mean?

00:00:15.690 --> 00:00:17.850
Well, a Florida federal
judge, Aileen Cannon,

00:00:17.850 --> 00:00:19.890
has temporarily halted
the criminal investigators

00:00:19.890 --> 00:00:21.150
from reviewing the records,

00:00:21.150 --> 00:00:22.200
founded MalMar-a-Lago

00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:23.220
and ordered the appointment

00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:25.590
of a special master to
check the government's work.

00:00:25.590 --> 00:00:26.880
However, the judge also allowed

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:29.940
the government's intelligence
damage assessment to continue.

00:00:29.940 --> 00:00:32.550
Many lawyers were quick to
condemn the judge's ruling.

00:00:32.550 --> 00:00:34.380
- I think the driver on
this from the beginning

00:00:34.380 --> 00:00:38.580
was loads of classified
information sitting in Mar-a-Lago.

00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:40.200
People say this was unprecedented.

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:41.960
Well, it's also
unprecedented for a president

00:00:41.960 --> 00:00:44.190
to take all this classified information

00:00:44.190 --> 00:00:45.540
and put 'em in a country club.

00:00:45.540 --> 00:00:46.692
- Are they right to be upset?

00:00:46.692 --> 00:00:48.600
Well, before we set our hair on fire,

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:49.740
let's look at how we got here

00:00:49.740 --> 00:00:52.170
and then consider what
the judge ruled and why.

00:00:52.170 --> 00:00:54.079
And of course, why the
DOJ is going to appeal

00:00:54.079 --> 00:00:57.043
that order to the 11th
Circuit and what that means.

00:00:57.043 --> 00:00:59.793
(majestic music)

00:01:01.350 --> 00:01:02.910
Now, all this got off to a strange start

00:01:02.910 --> 00:01:03.743
when Trump's lawyers

00:01:03.743 --> 00:01:06.360
didn't file this motion in the court

00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:08.760
that had created the search
warrant in the first place.

00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:11.790
Instead, they created a
new case, a civil case,

00:01:11.790 --> 00:01:14.910
and that landed in front of
Federal Judge Aileen Cannon,

00:01:14.910 --> 00:01:16.350
who we'll talk about in a moment.

00:01:16.350 --> 00:01:18.390
Now, in the DOJs brief in opposition

00:01:18.390 --> 00:01:20.100
to an appointment of a special master,

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:22.470
it outlined new details about
the classified documents

00:01:22.470 --> 00:01:24.660
that Trump had taken from the White House.

00:01:24.660 --> 00:01:26.520
First, it included a
photograph of the materials

00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:28.980
displayed on the floor of Mar-a-Lago.

00:01:28.980 --> 00:01:31.470
The photo shows five yellow
folders marked top secret,

00:01:31.470 --> 00:01:32.910
and a red one labeled secret,

00:01:32.910 --> 00:01:35.400
lying on the ground beside
a box of magazine covers.

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:36.930
Now, the photo was
pretty standard procedure

00:01:36.930 --> 00:01:37.763
by law enforcement.

00:01:37.763 --> 00:01:40.140
They normally document the
things that they have seized.

00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:42.300
Now, Trump and his lawyers
were outraged by the photo,

00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:43.380
which they say merely shows

00:01:43.380 --> 00:01:45.630
that Trump was in possession
of his own records,

00:01:45.630 --> 00:01:47.190
but also their response did not address

00:01:47.190 --> 00:01:49.440
one of the key claims that Trump
supporters continue to make

00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:52.394
that Trump declassified
everything in their possession.

00:01:52.394 --> 00:01:54.540
So far, the lawyers have not (chuckles)

00:01:54.540 --> 00:01:57.090
bothered to make this
argument in front of a court.

00:01:57.090 --> 00:01:58.350
But Donald Trump was very offended

00:01:58.350 --> 00:02:00.330
by this photograph for other reasons.

00:02:00.330 --> 00:02:02.160
He called up a conservative radio show

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:04.768
to rant about how sloppy the
FBI was with the records.

00:02:04.768 --> 00:02:08.610
He claimed he kept them
in a much neater fashion.

00:02:08.610 --> 00:02:10.770
- [Trump] But I had confidential documents

00:02:10.770 --> 00:02:13.320
spread out all over my floor,

00:02:13.320 --> 00:02:15.570
and like a slob.

00:02:15.570 --> 00:02:16.403
- Trump's family members

00:02:16.403 --> 00:02:18.330
also emphasized the former
president was a neat freak.

00:02:18.330 --> 00:02:20.400
Quote, "He's a very neat individual.

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:21.930
He would never scatter documents

00:02:21.930 --> 00:02:23.670
around on a floor like that."

00:02:23.670 --> 00:02:26.010
But none of this is about
whether Trump is a slobe.

00:02:26.010 --> 00:02:28.500
The issue is whether Trump
had possession of documents

00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:30.120
that he was not allowed to possess

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:32.103
in a completely unsecured location.

00:02:32.103 --> 00:02:34.830
Trump's comments could also
be viewed as an admission

00:02:34.830 --> 00:02:37.320
that those records were
not planted by the FBI,

00:02:37.320 --> 00:02:39.480
which is contrary some
of the other messaging

00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:41.190
that the Trump camp has been doing.

00:02:41.190 --> 00:02:42.270
The Justice Department's filing

00:02:42.270 --> 00:02:43.800
also pushed back on Trump's claim

00:02:43.800 --> 00:02:46.080
that he'd been cooperating
with government all along.

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:47.820
The DOJ said that when they arrived

00:02:47.820 --> 00:02:49.290
at Mar-a-Lago on June 3rd,

00:02:49.290 --> 00:02:51.060
one of Trump's lawyers
handed over a single

00:02:51.060 --> 00:02:53.790
red welled envelope,
double wrapped in tape.

00:02:53.790 --> 00:02:55.620
The lawyers said that the records inside

00:02:55.620 --> 00:02:57.330
had come from a storage room.

00:02:57.330 --> 00:02:59.430
Another lawyer, probably Christina Bobb,

00:02:59.430 --> 00:03:00.690
signed a certification letter,

00:03:00.690 --> 00:03:03.450
swearing under oath
that a diligence search

00:03:03.450 --> 00:03:06.180
had been conducted and that
all the classified materials

00:03:06.180 --> 00:03:08.070
on the property had been turned over,

00:03:08.070 --> 00:03:11.207
or all the documents that had
been labeled as classified.

00:03:11.207 --> 00:03:15.084
Side note, Alina Habba and
Christina Bobb, Trump's lawyers,

00:03:15.084 --> 00:03:17.040
have given several interviews

00:03:17.040 --> 00:03:18.720
where they potentially
incriminated themselves

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:21.440
as witnesses or targets of
the criminal investigation.

00:03:21.440 --> 00:03:22.620
- Sorry to interrupt.

00:03:22.620 --> 00:03:24.870
But was there a limited number of people

00:03:24.870 --> 00:03:27.210
who had access to that storage room?

00:03:27.210 --> 00:03:28.950
It seems like a pretty
important room, right?

00:03:28.950 --> 00:03:30.480
Since they've made such a big deal of it

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:31.350
and have the lock on it
- Right.

00:03:31.350 --> 00:03:32.183
- and so forth.

00:03:32.183 --> 00:03:33.720
So, were there a limited number of people

00:03:33.720 --> 00:03:35.223
who could go in and out?

00:03:36.180 --> 00:03:37.440
- Yes, absolutely.
- Or have access

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:39.360
to it.
- Truly. Mar-a-Lago...

00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:42.570
Certainly. Mar-a-Lago is
secure in and of itself.

00:03:42.570 --> 00:03:45.390
Just getting onto the compound is hard.

00:03:45.390 --> 00:03:47.610
And then, it was a locked door

00:03:47.610 --> 00:03:51.150
and getting backed down into
the basement, there's security.

00:03:51.150 --> 00:03:52.678
You can't just walk down there.

00:03:52.678 --> 00:03:54.630
Only certain members
of staff can get there.

00:03:54.630 --> 00:03:56.610
And then, there's only one key.

00:03:56.610 --> 00:03:59.790
- They found these three mundane statutes

00:03:59.790 --> 00:04:02.970
espionage and the two others, obstruction.

00:04:02.970 --> 00:04:06.330
- Now, Habba told Charlie
Kirk that on May 5th, 2022.

00:04:06.330 --> 00:04:08.040
She searched all desks, drawers,

00:04:08.040 --> 00:04:10.320
nightstands, dressers, closets, et cetera,

00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:12.180
with explicit permission from Trump,

00:04:12.180 --> 00:04:15.030
in an effort to track down
documents related to a subpoena

00:04:15.030 --> 00:04:17.910
from New York attorney general
Letitia James' investigation

00:04:17.910 --> 00:04:19.230
into the Trump organization.

00:04:19.230 --> 00:04:20.390
And although the New York investigation

00:04:20.390 --> 00:04:21.480
is a separate matter,

00:04:21.480 --> 00:04:24.780
Habba placed herself at the
scene of the potential crime.

00:04:24.780 --> 00:04:26.250
And when the Justice Department delegation

00:04:26.250 --> 00:04:27.660
asked to see the storage room,

00:04:27.660 --> 00:04:29.880
a lawyer for Trump
explicitly prohibited them

00:04:29.880 --> 00:04:32.967
from opening or looking into
any of the other boxes inside.

00:04:32.967 --> 00:04:34.950
The government asserted
that this stopped them

00:04:34.950 --> 00:04:36.390
from confirming that no materials

00:04:36.390 --> 00:04:39.114
with classification markings
had been left behind.

00:04:39.114 --> 00:04:41.400
Investigators then discovered evidence

00:04:41.400 --> 00:04:43.980
that there were still classified
documents at Mar-a-Lago.

00:04:43.980 --> 00:04:46.140
And this probably came
from witness interviews.

00:04:46.140 --> 00:04:47.640
And after further investigation,

00:04:47.640 --> 00:04:49.618
the DOJ team said it had reason to believe

00:04:49.618 --> 00:04:51.847
the government records
had likely been quote,

00:04:51.847 --> 00:04:53.760
"Concealed and removed
from the storage room,"

00:04:53.760 --> 00:04:55.837
and that efforts may have
been taken to, quote,

00:04:55.837 --> 00:04:57.789
"obstruct the government's investigation."

00:04:57.789 --> 00:05:00.540
Then, the government decided
to apply for a search warrant.

00:05:00.540 --> 00:05:02.160
In the filing, the government said

00:05:02.160 --> 00:05:04.020
that when they went inside Mar-a-Lago,

00:05:04.020 --> 00:05:05.910
agents found more than twice the number

00:05:05.910 --> 00:05:07.200
of classified documents

00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:08.754
that Trump's lawyers
had handed over before

00:05:08.754 --> 00:05:10.650
when they allegedly had made

00:05:10.650 --> 00:05:12.480
their diligence search back in June,

00:05:12.480 --> 00:05:15.270
including three, that were
found not in the storage room,

00:05:15.270 --> 00:05:17.564
but in desks in the
form president's office.

00:05:17.564 --> 00:05:21.510
Now, the DOJs brief also included
two grand jury subpoenas.

00:05:21.510 --> 00:05:22.590
The first subpoena asked Trump

00:05:22.590 --> 00:05:24.540
to turn over the documents at Mar-a-Lago.

00:05:24.540 --> 00:05:27.563
And the second was for footage
from surveillance cameras.

00:05:27.563 --> 00:05:29.910
Mar-a-Lago footage would be used

00:05:29.910 --> 00:05:32.370
to track the movement of the
materials within the club.

00:05:32.370 --> 00:05:34.710
Now, I'm largely going to skip
over the Trump reply brief

00:05:34.710 --> 00:05:36.990
in response to the DOJs opposition.

00:05:36.990 --> 00:05:39.540
It's less of a legal document

00:05:39.540 --> 00:05:42.600
and more of just a
public relations attempt.

00:05:42.600 --> 00:05:44.040
It really doesn't
address any of the things

00:05:44.040 --> 00:05:45.960
that the judge wanted them to address.

00:05:45.960 --> 00:05:48.630
It fails to rebut any of
the arguments (chuckles)

00:05:48.630 --> 00:05:51.180
that the DOJ has put forward.

00:05:51.180 --> 00:05:53.070
And similarly, as we covered before,

00:05:53.070 --> 00:05:55.140
the judge teed up exactly the issues

00:05:55.140 --> 00:05:57.540
that she wanted the Trump team to address,

00:05:57.540 --> 00:05:59.523
and they failed to address those issues

00:05:59.523 --> 00:06:01.200
in either the moving papers

00:06:01.200 --> 00:06:03.510
for the special master
or in the reply brief.

00:06:03.510 --> 00:06:05.760
Effectively, their papers
were just placeholders,

00:06:05.760 --> 00:06:08.190
just getting something
in front of the judge.

00:06:08.190 --> 00:06:10.718
Though as we now know,
that's all they needed to do.

00:06:10.718 --> 00:06:12.330
As I alluded to earlier,

00:06:12.330 --> 00:06:14.430
the judge has now ruled in Trump's favor

00:06:14.430 --> 00:06:16.650
on the motion to appoint a special master.

00:06:16.650 --> 00:06:18.180
The order appointing a special master

00:06:18.180 --> 00:06:20.520
was issued by District
Court Judge Aileen Cannon,

00:06:20.520 --> 00:06:22.320
who, it's important to note,

00:06:22.320 --> 00:06:25.080
was nominated by Trump
to the bench in 2020.

00:06:25.080 --> 00:06:26.850
Judge Cannon was confirmed by the Senate

00:06:26.850 --> 00:06:28.770
by a vote of 56 to 21

00:06:28.770 --> 00:06:32.550
just days after Trump lost
the November, 2020 election.

00:06:32.550 --> 00:06:33.450
So, first things first,

00:06:33.450 --> 00:06:35.550
is it kosher for a judge to
rule on a criminal matter

00:06:35.550 --> 00:06:37.386
involving the president
who appointed them?

00:06:37.386 --> 00:06:39.840
Well, there's absolutely no
law saying that they can't.

00:06:39.840 --> 00:06:41.220
Generally, it's not a conflict of interest

00:06:41.220 --> 00:06:42.330
for judges to rule on matters

00:06:42.330 --> 00:06:44.310
involving the president
who appointed them.

00:06:44.310 --> 00:06:45.930
This happens fairly regularly.

00:06:45.930 --> 00:06:47.760
But that typically comes
up in the context of laws

00:06:47.760 --> 00:06:48.780
that the president enacts

00:06:48.780 --> 00:06:50.520
or executive orders that they issue,

00:06:50.520 --> 00:06:52.151
not in actual criminal matters

00:06:52.151 --> 00:06:55.140
that concern that particular president.

00:06:55.140 --> 00:06:56.190
All right, so with that out of the way,

00:06:56.190 --> 00:06:58.110
let's look at what the judge actually did.

00:06:58.110 --> 00:07:01.830
The first issue is the
judge's order on Rule 41 (g).

00:07:01.830 --> 00:07:03.960
Trump filed a motion seeking the return

00:07:03.960 --> 00:07:05.670
of privileged materials and property

00:07:05.670 --> 00:07:08.310
under federal rule of
criminal procedure, 41 (g).

00:07:08.310 --> 00:07:10.237
That rule allows a person, quote,

00:07:10.237 --> 00:07:12.270
"Aggrieved by an unlawful
search and seizure

00:07:12.270 --> 00:07:14.070
of property or by the deprivation

00:07:14.070 --> 00:07:16.440
of property to move for
the property's return."

00:07:16.440 --> 00:07:18.270
Now, since there's no
pending criminal case,

00:07:18.270 --> 00:07:21.870
Trump filed a motion as a
plaintiff in a civil case.

00:07:21.870 --> 00:07:23.310
That means he was trying to initiate

00:07:23.310 --> 00:07:25.830
an action in the pre-indictment stage.

00:07:25.830 --> 00:07:27.480
Now, in the case of Richie versus Smith,

00:07:27.480 --> 00:07:29.640
the 5th Circuit Court of Appeals
developed a four-part test

00:07:29.640 --> 00:07:30.630
to determine whether the government

00:07:30.630 --> 00:07:33.270
must return property in
a pre-indictment case.

00:07:33.270 --> 00:07:35.490
First, whether the motion
for return of property

00:07:35.490 --> 00:07:37.590
accurately alleges the government agents

00:07:37.590 --> 00:07:38.820
displayed a callous disregard

00:07:38.820 --> 00:07:40.020
for the rights of the plaintiff.

00:07:40.020 --> 00:07:42.210
Second, whether the plaintiff
has an individual interest

00:07:42.210 --> 00:07:45.030
in and need for the material
whose return he seeks.

00:07:45.030 --> 00:07:47.520
Third, whether the plaintiff
would be irreparably injured

00:07:47.520 --> 00:07:49.440
by the denial of the
return of the property.

00:07:49.440 --> 00:07:51.780
And fourth, whether the
plaintiff has an adequate

00:07:51.780 --> 00:07:54.420
remedy at law for the
redress of his grievance.

00:07:54.420 --> 00:07:56.520
Now, the rationale of the Richie
case is that the government

00:07:56.520 --> 00:07:58.920
should not be allowed to season
retain privileged documents

00:07:58.920 --> 00:08:00.480
in a pre-indictment setting.

00:08:00.480 --> 00:08:03.150
However, quote, "In order
for an owner of property

00:08:03.150 --> 00:08:06.000
to invoke Rule 41 (g), he must show

00:08:06.000 --> 00:08:07.590
that he had a possessory interest

00:08:07.590 --> 00:08:10.260
in the property seized by the government."

00:08:10.260 --> 00:08:12.270
Now, the DOJ had argued
that Trump cannot show

00:08:12.270 --> 00:08:14.040
he had a right to the
records that were seized,

00:08:14.040 --> 00:08:15.600
citing the Presidential Records Act,

00:08:15.600 --> 00:08:17.310
which says that presidential records

00:08:17.310 --> 00:08:20.670
are owned by the United States,
not by the former president.

00:08:20.670 --> 00:08:22.290
And during oral argument on the motion,

00:08:22.290 --> 00:08:23.400
the judge asked Trump's lawyer

00:08:23.400 --> 00:08:25.740
to address the second
and third Richie factors.

00:08:25.740 --> 00:08:27.990
Lawyer Jim Trusty, Trump's lawyer,

00:08:27.990 --> 00:08:29.310
replied that the entire dispute

00:08:29.310 --> 00:08:32.000
was akin to an argument
over overdue library books.

00:08:32.000 --> 00:08:34.477
Now, the judge described
Trump's motion as quote,

00:08:34.477 --> 00:08:36.847
"Somewhat convoluted,"
but concluded that, quote,

00:08:36.847 --> 00:08:39.060
"this filing is procedurally permissible

00:08:39.060 --> 00:08:40.920
and creates an action in equity."

00:08:40.920 --> 00:08:42.960
Though, there has been a lot of pushback

00:08:42.960 --> 00:08:45.630
about the court's jurisdiction
in this particular case.

00:08:45.630 --> 00:08:47.190
And in assessing the Richie factors,

00:08:47.190 --> 00:08:48.240
the judge said that the DOJ

00:08:48.240 --> 00:08:50.610
did not display callous
disregard for Trump's rights.

00:08:50.610 --> 00:08:52.530
So, the government came out
ahead on the first factor,

00:08:52.530 --> 00:08:54.240
but it was all downhill from there.

00:08:54.240 --> 00:08:55.260
As to the second factor,

00:08:55.260 --> 00:08:57.000
whether Trump has an
individual interest in

00:08:57.000 --> 00:08:58.470
and need for the material seized,

00:08:58.470 --> 00:09:00.360
the judge said that this
factor cut in Trump's favor

00:09:00.360 --> 00:09:01.728
since the DOJ by its own admission,

00:09:01.728 --> 00:09:03.840
had seized Trump's medical records,

00:09:03.840 --> 00:09:06.843
correspondence related to taxes,
and financial information.

00:09:06.843 --> 00:09:08.850
Judge Cannon says that this amounts

00:09:08.850 --> 00:09:10.208
to over 500 pages of documents,

00:09:10.208 --> 00:09:13.350
however, the government
seized over 11,000 documents,

00:09:13.350 --> 00:09:14.820
which means that the subset of documents

00:09:14.820 --> 00:09:16.800
that belongs to Trump
amounts to less than 5%

00:09:16.800 --> 00:09:18.870
of the record seized under the warrant.

00:09:18.870 --> 00:09:21.240
The DOJ argued that the
mixing of these personal items

00:09:21.240 --> 00:09:24.480
with other documents
is evidence of a crime

00:09:24.480 --> 00:09:27.210
and can have evidentiary
value in and of itself.

00:09:27.210 --> 00:09:29.040
If Trump stored his personal records

00:09:29.040 --> 00:09:31.440
with classified materials
and post-it notes,

00:09:31.440 --> 00:09:33.870
it could suggest something
about his mens rea,

00:09:33.870 --> 00:09:36.210
his mental state, for what he was doing

00:09:36.210 --> 00:09:38.640
with these classified
or protected documents.

00:09:38.640 --> 00:09:40.740
Now, Judge Cannon is a
former federal prosecutor,

00:09:40.740 --> 00:09:42.840
and she agreed that the
Justice Department's concern

00:09:42.840 --> 00:09:45.171
was valid, but she said that
Trump's interest was stronger.

00:09:45.171 --> 00:09:47.310
And the third factor is irreparable harm.

00:09:47.310 --> 00:09:48.420
To show irreparable harm,

00:09:48.420 --> 00:09:49.380
a plaintiff needs to demonstrate

00:09:49.380 --> 00:09:51.240
that there would be no
remedy for the damage

00:09:51.240 --> 00:09:52.258
that the government inflicted.

00:09:52.258 --> 00:09:54.420
The judge concluded that a special master

00:09:54.420 --> 00:09:56.940
was necessary to stop harm
to Trump's reputation.

00:09:56.940 --> 00:09:59.010
Leaks to the press could
be harmful to Trump.

00:09:59.010 --> 00:10:01.080
And the judge concluded with no analysis

00:10:01.080 --> 00:10:02.490
that the government leaked
stories to the press,

00:10:02.490 --> 00:10:04.170
although the government
obviously denied this,

00:10:04.170 --> 00:10:05.550
and she seemed to ignore the fact

00:10:05.550 --> 00:10:07.440
that it was former president Trump,

00:10:07.440 --> 00:10:10.890
who was providing almost all
of the information to the press

00:10:10.890 --> 00:10:12.737
when these things happened in real time.

00:10:12.737 --> 00:10:14.910
Now, Judge Cannon said that Trump

00:10:14.910 --> 00:10:16.170
could be irreparably harmed,

00:10:16.170 --> 00:10:19.200
because of the stigma of
potential criminal prosecution,

00:10:19.200 --> 00:10:21.663
quote, "Frequently, the public
remembers the accusation

00:10:21.663 --> 00:10:24.900
and still suspects guilt,
even after an acquittal."

00:10:24.900 --> 00:10:27.960
Though as many criminal defense
lawyers have pointed out,

00:10:27.960 --> 00:10:31.436
including Renayo Mariotti,
Mark Zaid, and Sara Azari,

00:10:31.436 --> 00:10:33.930
all defendants suffer reputational damage

00:10:33.930 --> 00:10:35.932
when they are indicted,

00:10:35.932 --> 00:10:38.533
not just this former president.

00:10:38.533 --> 00:10:40.980
These lawyers also said that
they were looking forward

00:10:40.980 --> 00:10:43.590
to citing this case on
behalf of their clients.

00:10:43.590 --> 00:10:45.750
And yes, it would be good
for criminal defendants

00:10:45.750 --> 00:10:47.297
if they could have a special master

00:10:47.297 --> 00:10:50.340
to review evidence seized from them

00:10:50.340 --> 00:10:53.610
in the pre-indictment stage,
in all criminal cases.

00:10:53.610 --> 00:10:54.870
And they would have a much better chance

00:10:54.870 --> 00:10:56.010
of avoiding criminal indictment

00:10:56.010 --> 00:10:57.960
if they could identify
the government's witnesses

00:10:57.960 --> 00:10:59.789
and knew what the government
was thinking beforehand.

00:10:59.789 --> 00:11:01.890
And that's one of the reasons why the DOJ

00:11:01.890 --> 00:11:03.990
opposed Trump's motion
for a special master.

00:11:03.990 --> 00:11:05.610
But national security
lawyer, Bradley Moss,

00:11:05.610 --> 00:11:07.200
thinks that it won't help
most criminal defendants

00:11:07.200 --> 00:11:10.170
because the judge wrote this
to help Trump and Trump only.

00:11:10.170 --> 00:11:11.400
Now, in her order, Judge Cannon

00:11:11.400 --> 00:11:12.990
seems to confirm Moss' conclusion

00:11:12.990 --> 00:11:15.840
that the rules are different
in this particular case,

00:11:15.840 --> 00:11:18.390
quote, "As a function of
plaintiff's former position

00:11:18.390 --> 00:11:19.950
as president of the United States,

00:11:19.950 --> 00:11:22.350
the stigma associated
with the subject seizure

00:11:22.350 --> 00:11:23.820
is in a league of its own."

00:11:23.820 --> 00:11:25.530
And Judge Cannon came
up with another reason

00:11:25.530 --> 00:11:27.817
why Trump might suffer
irreparable harm, quote,

00:11:27.817 --> 00:11:30.210
"A future indictment based
to any degree on property

00:11:30.210 --> 00:11:33.120
that ought to be returned, would
result in reputational harm

00:11:33.120 --> 00:11:35.694
of a decidedly different
order of magnitude."

00:11:35.694 --> 00:11:37.290
Of course we aren't there yet.

00:11:37.290 --> 00:11:38.550
Trump has not been indicted,

00:11:38.550 --> 00:11:40.440
much less indicted because
of personal property

00:11:40.440 --> 00:11:42.190
that the FBI had no right to seize.

00:11:42.190 --> 00:11:45.000
And as many have pointed out, often,

00:11:45.000 --> 00:11:46.740
you are allowed to seize documents

00:11:46.740 --> 00:11:48.871
that are intermingled with the documents

00:11:48.871 --> 00:11:51.450
that are explicitly mentioned
in a search warrant.

00:11:51.450 --> 00:11:52.800
But the judge suggests that Trump

00:11:52.800 --> 00:11:54.900
wouldn't have any remedy or any defense

00:11:54.900 --> 00:11:56.280
if he was indicted based on material

00:11:56.280 --> 00:11:58.170
the government should not have seized.

00:11:58.170 --> 00:11:59.340
But that's incorrect.

00:11:59.340 --> 00:12:01.140
If Trump is indicted using evidence

00:12:01.140 --> 00:12:02.460
that the government should not have taken,

00:12:02.460 --> 00:12:04.873
his lawyers would file a motion
to suppress the evidence.

00:12:04.873 --> 00:12:06.810
But Judge Cannon heads this off

00:12:06.810 --> 00:12:08.012
and argues that a special master

00:12:08.012 --> 00:12:09.390
can prevent this from happening

00:12:09.390 --> 00:12:11.280
by checking the government's
work in the first place.

00:12:11.280 --> 00:12:12.660
Now, all of this seems to contradict

00:12:12.660 --> 00:12:13.800
the judge's same decision

00:12:13.800 --> 00:12:16.560
to allow the intelligence
community through the ODNI

00:12:16.560 --> 00:12:18.420
to continue its review of the evidence

00:12:18.420 --> 00:12:21.378
while the criminal
investigation has to stop.

00:12:21.378 --> 00:12:25.620
Here, the ODNI is doing
its damage assessment

00:12:25.620 --> 00:12:26.880
to find out who had access

00:12:26.880 --> 00:12:28.920
to the sensitive
information in the documents

00:12:28.920 --> 00:12:31.409
and whether national security
interests were compromised.

00:12:31.409 --> 00:12:33.390
And I suppose someone could argue

00:12:33.390 --> 00:12:35.045
that the criminal investigation

00:12:35.045 --> 00:12:38.310
into Trump's records
would harm his reputation

00:12:38.310 --> 00:12:40.950
while the intelligence community
investigation would not.

00:12:40.950 --> 00:12:42.600
But that seems incredibly dubious,

00:12:42.600 --> 00:12:45.990
not to mention it's all part
of the same executive branch,

00:12:45.990 --> 00:12:48.990
which many have argued, as
a unitary executive branch.

00:12:48.990 --> 00:12:49.823
And on top of that,

00:12:49.823 --> 00:12:50.880
the intelligence review

00:12:50.880 --> 00:12:53.274
could lead to further criminal charges.

00:12:53.274 --> 00:12:55.650
Moreover, some people
have already concluded

00:12:55.650 --> 00:12:58.140
that Trump put human
intelligence sources at risk.

00:12:58.140 --> 00:12:59.989
That conclusion, if it's mistaken,

00:12:59.989 --> 00:13:02.760
would seem to be just
as serious as the stigma

00:13:02.760 --> 00:13:05.310
that attaches to a criminal
investigation itself.

00:13:05.310 --> 00:13:07.140
Now, the DOJ filed an extensive brief

00:13:07.140 --> 00:13:09.100
explaining how its filter
team separates personal items

00:13:09.100 --> 00:13:11.640
and attorney client privileged records

00:13:11.640 --> 00:13:13.890
from everything else that the DOJ sees.

00:13:13.890 --> 00:13:16.020
The judge was unmoved by
their arguments however.

00:13:16.020 --> 00:13:19.234
She held that Trump met
the four Richie factors.

00:13:19.234 --> 00:13:21.900
The judge said that
rating the ex-president

00:13:21.900 --> 00:13:23.970
without a third party reviewer
just doesn't look good.

00:13:23.970 --> 00:13:25.350
Quote, "The government's argument

00:13:25.350 --> 00:13:27.000
that another round of initial screening

00:13:27.000 --> 00:13:29.100
is unnecessary also disregards the value

00:13:29.100 --> 00:13:31.230
added by an outside reviewer in terms of,

00:13:31.230 --> 00:13:33.654
at a minimum, the appearance of fairness.

00:13:33.654 --> 00:13:36.630
Even if the DOJ filter review teams

00:13:36.630 --> 00:13:38.880
often pass procedural muster,

00:13:38.880 --> 00:13:39.990
they're not always perceived

00:13:39.990 --> 00:13:42.390
to be as impartial as special masters."

00:13:42.390 --> 00:13:44.100
And that takes us to the judge's analysis

00:13:44.100 --> 00:13:45.900
of the executive privilege issue,

00:13:45.900 --> 00:13:48.153
which is to put it mildly, convoluted.

00:13:48.153 --> 00:13:50.730
Judge Cannon says the
government made a mistake

00:13:50.730 --> 00:13:53.326
by not screening the records
for executive privilege.

00:13:53.326 --> 00:13:55.290
Now, this overlooks the
fact that Justice Department

00:13:55.290 --> 00:13:56.670
is part of the executive branch

00:13:56.670 --> 00:14:00.090
and that President Biden holds
the executive privilege now

00:14:00.090 --> 00:14:02.490
and has already delegated
his authority to NARA,

00:14:02.490 --> 00:14:04.260
which says none of these documents

00:14:04.260 --> 00:14:05.836
can be protected by Trump

00:14:05.836 --> 00:14:08.280
under the guise of executive privilege.

00:14:08.280 --> 00:14:10.050
But this order hints that
this is really dispute

00:14:10.050 --> 00:14:12.600
between former president Trump
and current president Biden.

00:14:12.600 --> 00:14:13.710
The Supreme Court's president says

00:14:13.710 --> 00:14:16.770
that executive privilege almost
under every circumstance,

00:14:16.770 --> 00:14:19.260
stays with the office of
the current president.

00:14:19.260 --> 00:14:22.709
It's theoretically possible
under the Nixon versus GSA case

00:14:22.709 --> 00:14:24.540
for a former president to have

00:14:24.540 --> 00:14:26.980
some tiny sliver of executive privilege,

00:14:26.980 --> 00:14:29.910
but that's never happened so far

00:14:29.910 --> 00:14:30.990
and the circumstances at which

00:14:30.990 --> 00:14:34.920
that could theoretically happen
are extremely circumscribed.

00:14:34.920 --> 00:14:36.780
But the judge says that a special master

00:14:36.780 --> 00:14:38.070
must review all the government records

00:14:38.070 --> 00:14:39.886
to determine if Trump can
raise executive privilege

00:14:39.886 --> 00:14:41.610
to shield these records.

00:14:41.610 --> 00:14:43.620
But so far, the judge has
only raised this issue.

00:14:43.620 --> 00:14:46.170
We don't know what procedures,
if any of the special master

00:14:46.170 --> 00:14:48.900
will have to follow when one is appointed.

00:14:48.900 --> 00:14:50.190
So, let's talk about what it means

00:14:50.190 --> 00:14:51.630
to appoint a special master.

00:14:51.630 --> 00:14:53.790
Normally, a special master
would help the court

00:14:53.790 --> 00:14:54.690
sift through materials

00:14:54.690 --> 00:14:56.742
that may be subject to
attorney-client privilege.

00:14:56.742 --> 00:14:59.640
Now, someone is going
to screen these records

00:14:59.640 --> 00:15:01.140
for attorney-client privilege

00:15:01.140 --> 00:15:03.210
and presumably, executive privilege.

00:15:03.210 --> 00:15:04.470
But that's sort of up in the air.

00:15:04.470 --> 00:15:05.970
The federal courts
haven't developed case law

00:15:05.970 --> 00:15:09.420
about when an ex-president can
assert executive privilege.

00:15:09.420 --> 00:15:11.806
So, the special master is
not gonna have any guidance

00:15:11.806 --> 00:15:13.751
based on the case law itself.

00:15:13.751 --> 00:15:16.500
But presumably, we'll get
more guidance from the court

00:15:16.500 --> 00:15:17.910
when it issues its final order,

00:15:17.910 --> 00:15:20.310
actually appointing the special master.

00:15:20.310 --> 00:15:21.930
And there is another complication

00:15:21.930 --> 00:15:23.340
that goes unaddressed by the judge.

00:15:23.340 --> 00:15:26.250
Here, the PRA requires a dispute
over presidential records

00:15:26.250 --> 00:15:29.391
to be handled in the US district
court in Washington, DC.

00:15:29.391 --> 00:15:32.377
44 USC section 2204 states, quote,

00:15:32.377 --> 00:15:33.480
"The United States district court

00:15:33.480 --> 00:15:36.090
for the district of Columbia
shall have jurisdiction

00:15:36.090 --> 00:15:38.310
over any action initiated
by the former president,

00:15:38.310 --> 00:15:39.540
asserting that a determination

00:15:39.540 --> 00:15:41.130
made by the archivist violates

00:15:41.130 --> 00:15:43.666
the former president's
constitutional rights or privileges."

00:15:43.666 --> 00:15:46.530
Here, Judge Cannon, in her order,

00:15:46.530 --> 00:15:50.160
said she recognizes that
this is the law of the PRA,

00:15:50.160 --> 00:15:52.980
but she doesn't say how the
special master's appointment

00:15:52.980 --> 00:15:55.166
can be squared with Section 2204.

00:15:55.166 --> 00:15:57.780
If she admits that Section 2204 applies,

00:15:57.780 --> 00:15:59.460
then why would a special master appointed

00:15:59.460 --> 00:16:01.590
in the Southern District
of Florida have any power?

00:16:01.590 --> 00:16:04.500
Why does her particular civil court case

00:16:04.500 --> 00:16:07.230
have any power in the
Southern District of Florida?

00:16:07.230 --> 00:16:08.700
So, what happens next?

00:16:08.700 --> 00:16:10.110
Well, now, the parties
must meet and confer

00:16:10.110 --> 00:16:11.843
about who might serve as a special master.

00:16:11.843 --> 00:16:15.240
Ideally, they would
agree on a list of names,

00:16:15.240 --> 00:16:16.995
but that's almost certainly
not going to happen here.

00:16:16.995 --> 00:16:18.660
Keep in mind that this order

00:16:18.660 --> 00:16:20.610
only applies to evidence
seized on August 8th.

00:16:20.610 --> 00:16:22.830
It actually doesn't apply to
an earlier subset of documents

00:16:22.830 --> 00:16:25.290
that Trump sent to NARA
or the boxes of records

00:16:25.290 --> 00:16:28.650
that his lawyers gave to the
New York City DA, Alvin Bragg.

00:16:28.650 --> 00:16:29.550
And while many attorneys

00:16:29.550 --> 00:16:31.778
have rightfully criticized
Judge Cannon's order here,

00:16:31.778 --> 00:16:33.480
we'll really get a window

00:16:33.480 --> 00:16:35.820
into whether she's just
trying to bend over backwards

00:16:35.820 --> 00:16:37.920
to provide fairness to a former president

00:16:37.920 --> 00:16:39.560
in a politically charged atmosphere,

00:16:39.560 --> 00:16:41.460
or whether she's in the bag for Trump

00:16:41.460 --> 00:16:44.250
based on who she appoints
as the special master.

00:16:44.250 --> 00:16:45.570
And on Thursday, the Department of Justice

00:16:45.570 --> 00:16:47.730
officially filed a notice
of appeal with Judge Cannon

00:16:47.730 --> 00:16:49.650
and a motion for partial stay of her order

00:16:49.650 --> 00:16:52.380
regarding the special
master pending that appeal.

00:16:52.380 --> 00:16:54.180
Now, the notice of appeal
is just a placeholder.

00:16:54.180 --> 00:16:55.890
It's just a paragraph that
you file with the court

00:16:55.890 --> 00:16:57.480
that says they will appeal

00:16:57.480 --> 00:17:00.629
the September 5th special
master order and the injunction,

00:17:00.629 --> 00:17:03.300
but the motion for partial
stay has more detail.

00:17:03.300 --> 00:17:04.133
The government, quote,

00:17:04.133 --> 00:17:05.820
"Seeks to stay to the
extent that the order

00:17:05.820 --> 00:17:07.050
enjoins the further review

00:17:07.050 --> 00:17:09.720
and use for criminal
investigative purposes of records,

00:17:09.720 --> 00:17:11.010
bearing classification markings

00:17:11.010 --> 00:17:12.150
that were recovered pursuant

00:17:12.150 --> 00:17:13.680
to a court authorized search warrant,

00:17:13.680 --> 00:17:14.790
and requires the government

00:17:14.790 --> 00:17:16.380
to disclose those classified records

00:17:16.380 --> 00:17:17.733
to a special master for review."

00:17:17.733 --> 00:17:19.860
Now, the DOJ says that it disagrees

00:17:19.860 --> 00:17:21.600
with the court's decision
to appoint a special master

00:17:21.600 --> 00:17:22.433
over the materials,

00:17:22.433 --> 00:17:24.330
but it's only seeking an immediate relief

00:17:24.330 --> 00:17:26.070
over a subset of those records,

00:17:26.070 --> 00:17:27.600
the ones marked classified,

00:17:27.600 --> 00:17:29.880
because those aspects of the order

00:17:29.880 --> 00:17:31.830
will cause the most
immediate and serious harms

00:17:31.830 --> 00:17:33.510
to the government and the public.

00:17:33.510 --> 00:17:35.100
The classified records, a discrete set

00:17:35.100 --> 00:17:37.350
of just over 100 documents,
have already been segregated

00:17:37.350 --> 00:17:38.520
from the other seized records,

00:17:38.520 --> 00:17:40.560
and are being maintained separately.

00:17:40.560 --> 00:17:42.900
Now, the Department of Justice
argues the classified records

00:17:42.900 --> 00:17:44.953
are also not personal to the plaintiff,

00:17:44.953 --> 00:17:46.350
former president Trump,

00:17:46.350 --> 00:17:48.481
and would not reveal any
sensitive personal information.

00:17:48.481 --> 00:17:49.920
The Justice Department took issue

00:17:49.920 --> 00:17:51.879
with the judge's conclusion
that as of right now,

00:17:51.879 --> 00:17:54.720
it is impossible to determine
which records Trump might own,

00:17:54.720 --> 00:17:56.157
saying that the classified
government records,

00:17:56.157 --> 00:17:58.195
"Are easily identifiable
by their markings,

00:17:58.195 --> 00:18:01.950
are already segregated from
the other seized records

00:18:01.950 --> 00:18:03.510
and do not include personal records

00:18:03.510 --> 00:18:04.980
or potentially privileged communications

00:18:04.980 --> 00:18:06.165
with his personal attorneys."

00:18:06.165 --> 00:18:08.587
In other words, the DOJ has
already done his review.

00:18:08.587 --> 00:18:10.590
The government disagreed, as expected,

00:18:10.590 --> 00:18:12.990
with the judge's interpretation
of executive privilege

00:18:12.990 --> 00:18:15.047
and the Nixon versus GSA case.

00:18:15.047 --> 00:18:17.550
And the DOJ has a bone
to pick with Judge Cannon

00:18:17.550 --> 00:18:20.730
over her reliance on a
concurrence by Justice Kavanaugh

00:18:20.730 --> 00:18:22.293
in the case of Trump versus Thompson.

00:18:22.293 --> 00:18:24.007
The DOJ argued, quote,

00:18:24.007 --> 00:18:26.070
"Thompson involved a claim
of executive privilege

00:18:26.070 --> 00:18:27.240
by a plaintiff as to records

00:18:27.240 --> 00:18:29.280
sought by a congressional committee,

00:18:29.280 --> 00:18:31.590
which means that he wanted
to stop dissemination

00:18:31.590 --> 00:18:32.700
of the privileged materials

00:18:32.700 --> 00:18:34.560
outside of the executive branch."

00:18:34.560 --> 00:18:36.210
And that makes a little bit of sense

00:18:36.210 --> 00:18:38.190
in the possible theoretical places

00:18:38.190 --> 00:18:41.456
where a former president could
exert executive privilege,

00:18:41.456 --> 00:18:43.590
it would probably be
against a third party,

00:18:43.590 --> 00:18:45.270
not the executive branch itself.

00:18:45.270 --> 00:18:46.987
And the DOJ argued that, quote,

00:18:46.987 --> 00:18:48.480
"Neither the Supreme Court's opinion

00:18:48.480 --> 00:18:49.890
and Kavanaugh's concurring statement,

00:18:49.890 --> 00:18:51.840
concluded that an ex-president

00:18:51.840 --> 00:18:54.060
can successfully assert
executive privilege

00:18:54.060 --> 00:18:56.430
to prevent the executive branch itself

00:18:56.430 --> 00:18:59.070
from reviewing and using its own records."

00:18:59.070 --> 00:19:01.080
The DOJ also argued it
didn't make any sense

00:19:01.080 --> 00:19:02.250
that part of the executive branch

00:19:02.250 --> 00:19:03.210
could continue its review

00:19:03.210 --> 00:19:05.370
and part of the executive
branch could not.

00:19:05.370 --> 00:19:06.630
But this appeal is a risky move.

00:19:06.630 --> 00:19:08.340
It could lead to a delay
that could take longer

00:19:08.340 --> 00:19:09.973
than complying with a
special master procedures.

00:19:09.973 --> 00:19:11.820
And they could also lose the appeal

00:19:11.820 --> 00:19:13.800
because the 11th Circuit
is gonna 11th Circuit

00:19:13.800 --> 00:19:15.810
and generate unfavorable precedent.

00:19:15.810 --> 00:19:16.770
And in a minute order,

00:19:16.770 --> 00:19:19.197
the court asked both parties
to respond to the DOJs motion

00:19:19.197 --> 00:19:23.220
and it gave Trump until September
12th to file his response.

00:19:23.220 --> 00:19:24.870
Now, some see this as the
beginning of the judge

00:19:24.870 --> 00:19:26.820
walking back from her extreme position

00:19:26.820 --> 00:19:28.654
before getting hammered on appeal,

00:19:28.654 --> 00:19:32.070
but she may also just be
crossing T's and dotting I's.

00:19:32.070 --> 00:19:34.170
But you'll be able to read all
about Trump's circumvention

00:19:34.170 --> 00:19:37.140
of all rational laws in
today's sponsor, Morning Brew,

00:19:37.140 --> 00:19:38.190
because that's my favorite way

00:19:38.190 --> 00:19:40.140
to catch up with pop culture and the news.

00:19:40.140 --> 00:19:41.892
I've actually been subscribed
to Morning Brew for forever.

00:19:41.892 --> 00:19:44.460
It's one of my absolute
